  "id": "Compound::DB00539 - Disease::DOID:1612",
  "verbalization": "Toremifene appears to be linked to Breast Cancer through a series of indirect pathways involving selective estrogen receptor modulators and antineoplastic treatments. The evidence suggests that Toremifene, as a selective estrogen receptor modulator, may influence cellular processes that contribute to the development of breast cancer, as indicated by its role in causing Uterine Polyps and Endometrial Cancer, both of which are associated with estrogen receptor activation. These conditions, in turn, may predispose individuals to breast cancer, as Tamoxifen and Anastrozole are known to treat breast cancer by inhibiting estrogen receptor activity. The shared ancestry of Toremifene with these compounds, through the Organic Aromatic Compound and Selective Estrogen Receptor Modulator categories, underscores a potential mechanism by which Toremifene could influence breast cancer progression. While the direct causal link between Toremifene and breast cancer remains indirect, the cumulative evidence suggests a plausible pathway through estrogen receptor modulation and its downstream effects on cellular behavior.",
